`@html` provides compile-time HTML markup helpers as a universal Core package with MothSource backing. The compiler reads ordinary Moth source from `packages/html/@mod.moth`. See @../../#package-origins-and-backing (Package origins and backing) for origin and backing rules.

`@html` ships as a skeleton. Its exports cover common HTML tags, formatting helpers and table components. Basic style inserts belong to `@styles`, while browser drawing stays in the builder-specific `@web/canvas` package.

### The fixed template prelude

Compile-time constants from `@html` and `@styles` are visible flat inside `.mtf` files without an explicit dependency clause. This is the fixed template prelude.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- docs/intro.mtf
    [p: A paragraph built from the @html p helper.]

    [codeblock, $code "moth": value = 42]
]

The prelude is compiler policy, not builder configuration. Every builder receives the official `@html` and `@styles` Core roots, and `.mtf` is compiler-supported under every builder. Both packages precede every `.mtf` consumer.

A `.moth` file that needs specific `@html` symbols binds them explicitly:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    @html table, th, td
]

A `.mtf` file has no implicit module facade constants. Reach any helper value with a qualified reference that names the helper path and one selected symbol, or move the shared value into a helper the template can select. Root-local declarations in `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth` and `@entry.moth` stay invisible. See @../../../moth-templates/#template-scope (Template scope) for the full template scope contract.

A qualified selection introduces no local binding, so it never collides with a prelude name. A real local binding that matches a prelude name still fails with an ordinary visible-name collision.

### Exported constants

All helpers are compile-time `#String` constants that produce HTML strings. They fold at compile time and produce no runtime code.

#### HTML tags

[table:
    [th:
        [: Helper]
        [: Produces]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `p`]
        [: `<p>` paragraph]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `h1` through `h6`]
        [: heading levels]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `small`]
        [: `<small>` text]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `large`]
        [: `<large>` text]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `img`]
        [: `<img>` with `src`, `alt` and `style` slots]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `div`]
        [: `<div>` container]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `span`]
        [: `<span>` inline container]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `canvas`]
        [: `<canvas>` with `id` and `style` slots]
    ]
]

#### Formatting helpers

[table:
    [th:
        [: Helper]
        [: Produces]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `bold`]
        [: `<b>` bold text]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `italic`]
        [: `<i>` italic text]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `underline`]
        [: `<u>` underlined text]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `strikethrough`]
        [: `<s>` strikethrough text]
    ]
]

#### Code and tables

[table:
    [th:
        [: Helper]
        [: Produces]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `codeblock`]
        [: `<pre>` code block]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `table`]
        [: styled `<table>` with header and row slots]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `th`]
        [: `<th scope="col">` header cell]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `td`]
        [: `<td>` data cell]
    ]
]

#### Utility style inserts from `@styles`

The `style` slots exposed by the helpers above accept the six inline fragments from the separate `@styles` Core package, including `center` and `larger`. See @../styles/ (@styles) for the export table and prelude rules.

### The `$code` directive

`$code` renders a balanced template body as an HTML code block with lexical
highlighting. The compiler owns the directive and its formatter, so it is
available under every builder, not only the HTML one.

Supported aliases:

- `text` / `txt`
- `html`
- `markdown` / `md`
- `toml`
- `json`
- `yaml` / `yml`
- `css`
- `c`
- `sql`
- `moth`
- `javascript` / `js`
- `typescript` / `ts`
- `python` / `py`
- `rust` / `rs`
- `bash` / `sh` / `shell`

[codeblock, $code "moth":
[codeblock, $code "moth":
    render |name String| -> String:
        return ["Hello, ", name]
    ;
]
]

`$code` escapes HTML-sensitive characters and wraps the body in
`<code class='codeblock'>`. Dynamic expression anchors pass through as sealed
content without highlighting or escaping. Highlighting is lexical presentation
only. It never validates the snippet, resolves names or reports diagnostics.
Every language profile shares the same general role palette, and the Moth
profile uses the compiler-owned source-word classification plus bounded lexical
roles for contracts, functions, directives, dependency paths and the `io` namespace.
Moth template bodies stay plain authored text around their highlighted heads and
nested templates. A body is not re-highlighted by the directive that will format it.

### Browser drawing

The `canvas` markup helper remains in `@html`. Drawing functions and opaque canvas handles belong to `@web/canvas`, which a web-capable builder must provide.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    @web/canvas get_canvas, context_2d, set_fill_style, fill_rect

    element = get_canvas("my-canvas")!
    context ~= context_2d(element)!

    set_fill_style(~context, "red")
    fill_rect(~context, 10.0, 10.0, 50.0, 50.0)
]

### Backend behaviour

The compile-time constants need no runtime assets. They fold into HTML strings during compilation.

Calls to `@web/canvas` emit JavaScript runtime assets when reachable. See @../../builder/canvas/ (@web/canvas) for the runtime asset contract.

### Unsupported source forms

- Direct symbol-path clauses such as `@html/p` face rejection. Use direct selections or a namespace clause.
- `@html` exposes no runtime canvas types. `Canvas2d` comes from `@web/canvas`.
- Only compile-time constants are visible inside `.mtf` files. Functions and types require an explicit dependency clause in `.moth` files.

### Deferred surfaces

`@html` ships as a skeleton. Future expansions include richer semantic tag coverage and standard slots. Higher-level layouts, components and additional style systems belong in separate packages. See @../../../progress (progress matrix) for current status.

